FUNCTIONS

Functions, in C# known as Methods, are snippets of independent code that execute a desired function upon being called. They serve as a way to avoid repetition with
procedures you find yourself doing often. Static pertains to the Method being of the class defined above it, but not an element of it. Void means that the Method's
completion wont return a value of any kind.

Its syntax normally looks like this:

class Program*
  {
    static void name-of-method()**
    {
    //code to execute
    }
  }

*Class does not strictly have to be Program.
**Important! Use ThisKindOfNamingSystem for Methods. This is for readability, and to juxtapose against the naming convetion of variables.

To 'call' (invoke a Method) in code, you need to type the name of your method followed by two parenthesis.

EX:

static void ShoutAtMe()
{
  Console.WriteLine("AAAAH! AAAAAAAAAH!  AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAH!");
}

ShoutAtMe() // would print out this ^

